E = [
("06-01","Marilyn Monroe's birthday and the art of the breathy mic","Pop","1926","Close-mic crooning only became possible with sensitive condenser microphones, letting singers whisper instead of project.\n\nBefore electrical recording, a singer's job was partly athletic. Acoustic recording required enough volume to physically move a diaphragm, and live performance in a large room required filling it without amplification — which is why the popular singers of the early twentieth century sound like opera singers whether or not they were trained as such.\n\nThe condenser microphone changed the arithmetic entirely. Suddenly a whisper could be captured with detail, and singers could work at conversational volume with all the nuance that permits: breath, consonants, the slight break in a held note. Bing Crosby was the first major figure to build a style on it, and everything called crooning descends from that technical shift.\n\nMonroe's singing voice is the extreme case. It has almost no projection and enormous intimacy, and it only functions as a recorded and amplified instrument. A hundred years earlier there would have been no way for it to exist professionally.\n\nThe broader point is that vocal style is downstream of microphone technology more often than of taste. Belting, crooning, close-mic rap delivery, and modern pop's heavily compressed intimacy are each responses to what the equipment of the period rewarded.",["Before electric mics, singers needed opera-level volume","The intimate vocal style is a technology story as much as a taste one"],"mic","story",{}),
("06-02","Charlie Watts kept time by leaving the hi-hat out","Rock","1941","The Rolling Stones drummer lifted his hi-hat hand on backbeats, a jazz habit that gave the band its slight, distinctive drag.\n\nThe technique is small and its consequences are not. Most rock drummers play continuous eighth notes on the hi-hat while the snare hits the backbeat, so both hands strike together on beats two and four. Watts lifted his right hand off the hi-hat at exactly those moments, so the snare lands alone.\n\nThe result is a fractional gap in the texture on every backbeat, which the ear reads as the beat pulling slightly rather than driving. It is most of why Stones grooves feel loose and slightly behind, and it is why drummers who play the parts note-perfectly still fail to sound like the records.\n\nHe took the habit from jazz, which is what he actually cared about. He described himself as a jazz drummer for his entire life, played in jazz ensembles throughout, and was reportedly bemused to have spent sixty years in a rock band.\n\nHis playing is also notably economical. He rarely fills, almost never solos, and leaves an enormous amount of space, which is what allows two guitars playing simultaneously to sit where they do.\n\nHe died in 2021, having played with essentially the same feel since 1963.",["He considered himself a jazz drummer his whole life","That tiny gap is why Stones grooves feel loose"],"drums","producer",{}),
("06-03","Which 'Twist and Shout' wins: Isley Brothers or Beatles?","Rock","1962","The Isley Brothers cut the definitive early version. Lennon recorded the Beatles' take last on a session day with a shredded throat.\n\nThe Isleys' 1962 recording is the one that established the song — call and response, building intensity, and a vocal arrangement that everything afterward copied. It was not the first version; the Top Notes cut it a year earlier in an arrangement Bert Berns, who co-wrote it, reportedly hated.\n\nThe Beatles recorded theirs at the end of a single day in which they had cut most of their debut album. Lennon had a cold, had been singing for something in the region of twelve hours, and his voice was going. It was scheduled last precisely because everyone knew what it would do to him.\n\nHe drank milk, gargled, and did it in one take. A second take was attempted and was unusable, because there was nothing left.\n\nThat is why the record sounds the way it does. The tearing quality in the vocal is not a stylistic choice or a studio effect — it is a voice being destroyed in real time, captured because there was no alternative.\n\nBoth versions are correct. The Isleys' is a great arrangement performed well. The Beatles' is a document of physical extremity, which is a different and rarer thing.",["Lennon's voice gave out immediately afterward","It was done in one take because he could not sing another"],"mic","versus",{"a":"The Isley Brothers (1962)","b":"The Beatles (1963)"}),
("06-04","Cecil Taylor turned the piano into percussion","Jazz","1929","Taylor attacked the keyboard with clusters and forearms, treating it as 88 tuned drums rather than a melodic instrument.\n\nThe reconception is total. Conventional piano technique treats the instrument as a means of producing lines and harmonies; Taylor treated it as a percussion instrument that happens to have pitches, using tone clusters, forearm strikes, and extraordinary physical velocity to produce dense masses of sound rather than melodies.\n\nHe was conservatory trained, at the New England Conservatory, and had a thorough grounding in European modernism as well as jazz. The playing is not an absence of technique; it is an enormous amount of technique pointed somewhere unfamiliar.\n\nAudiences left. Early performances in the fifties and sixties emptied rooms routinely, and he spent years working day jobs — including as a dishwasher — while making some of the most demanding music of the period.\n\nHis endurance was legendary. Solo performances could run over an hour without pause at a physical intensity closer to athletics than to recital playing.\n\nHe is one of the founding figures of free jazz alongside Ornette Coleman, and unlike Coleman he never made a concession toward accessibility in six decades.\n\nHe died in 2018, having been essentially uncompromising for longer than most careers last.",["He trained classically at conservatory","Audiences frequently walked out of early performances"],"piano","producer",{}),
("06-05","Bill Withers recorded 'Grandma's Hands' in under two minutes","Soul","1971","Withers wrote about his grandmother's church in West Virginia. The song is barely 120 seconds long and needed nothing more.\n\nThe restraint is the craft. It is a series of images of one woman's hands — clapping in church, picking him up, warning him about the street — and each verse is a single memory delivered without commentary. There is no bridge, no key change, and no attempt to broaden it into a statement about grandmothers generally.\n\nHe resisted adding anything. The song says what it has to say and stops, which is a much harder decision than extending it.\n\nWithers was in his early thirties and still working a factory job when he recorded it, and the writing throughout that first album has a plainness that came from not having spent years inside the music industry learning what songs are supposed to do.\n\nBlackstreet's 'No Diggity' is built on it, using the guitar figure and Withers's voice, and it introduced the song to an audience two generations later.\n\nThe original runs about two minutes. Contemporary radio convention said a single needed roughly three, and Withers's response was that the song was finished.\n\nHe left the industry entirely in the mid-eighties and declined, for the rest of his life, to be persuaded back.",["Blackstreet later built 'No Diggity' on its bones","He resisted adding a bridge, saying it was finished"],"guitar","sample",{"src":"Bill Withers - Grandma's Hands (1971)","hits":["Blackstreet - No Diggity","Various nineties R&B records","Live hip-hop interpolations"]}),
("06-06","Prince Buster shaped the beat that became ska","Reggae","1938","Buster instructed guitarists to accent the offbeat, flipping Jamaican R&B into ska and eventually rocksteady and reggae.\n\nThe instruction was specific and consequential. Jamaican musicians in the late fifties were largely playing American R&B, which accents beats two and four. Buster told his guitarist to play on the upbeat instead — the and between the beats — which inverts the rhythmic feel entirely and produces the chopping skank that defines ska.\n\nEverything downstream follows from that single displacement. Ska slowed into rocksteady, rocksteady slowed further into reggae, and the offbeat guitar remained constant throughout.\n\nBuster was also a sound system operator, and the clashes he staged against rivals — particularly Coxsone Dodd and Duke Reid — were public theatre with real stakes, involving exclusive dubplates, insult records aimed at opponents by name, and occasional violence.\n\nHis records were carried to Britain by Jamaican migrants and became foundational to mod and skinhead culture in the sixties, and then to the two-tone movement in the late seventies. The Specials, Madness, and the Beat rebuilt substantial parts of his catalogue — Madness took their name from one of his songs and recorded a tribute to him as their debut single.\n\nHe died in 2016.",["He staged sound-system clashes as public theatre","Two-tone bands in Britain rebuilt his catalogue"],"guitar","producer",{}),
("06-07","Prince changed his name to a symbol to escape a contract","Funk","1993","In a dispute with Warner Bros., Prince adopted an unpronounceable glyph and wrote 'slave' on his face at appearances.\n\nThe dispute was about ownership and pace. Prince recorded faster than Warner wanted to release — the label had a commercial interest in spacing albums out and protecting the value of each — and he did not own his master recordings, which meant the catalogue he had made belonged to someone else.\n\nThe symbol was a legal manoeuvre as much as a gesture. His contract obligated Prince to deliver albums; an artist with no pronounceable name and a different legal identity complicated the label's ability to market him and made the situation actively expensive.\n\nWarner mailed floppy disks to the press containing a custom font so publications could reproduce the glyph. Most gave up and wrote 'the artist formerly known as Prince'.\n\nWriting 'slave' on his cheek at public appearances was widely mocked at the time, including by people who thought a multi-millionaire had chosen an obscene comparison. The substantive point underneath — that standard recording contracts transferred ownership of an artist's life's work in perpetuity — was one the industry has since had to concede repeatedly.\n\nHe regained his masters in 2014. Artists renegotiating master ownership now are working ground he cleared at considerable reputational cost.",["Media resorted to calling him 'the artist formerly known as'","Floppy disks with the symbol font were mailed to press"],"guitar","story",{}),
("06-08","Brooklyn's Kings Theatre reopening drew a Flatbush crowd","Brooklyn","2015","After decades dark, the Flatbush palace reopened with a sold-out show, restoring a major venue to the neighborhood's doorstep.\n\nThe restoration took roughly four years and a budget in the region of ninety-five million dollars, funded through a mix of public money and private investment. The building had been closed since 1977 and had taken serious water damage, so a great deal of the ornamental plasterwork had to be recast from surviving fragments rather than repaired.\n\nWhat reopened is one of the largest theatres in Brooklyn, seating around three thousand, roughly a mile from Cortelyou Road.\n\nThe practical effect on the neighbourhood was immediate. For nearly four decades, an arena- or theatre-scale act playing New York meant a trip to Manhattan or downtown Brooklyn, and Central Brooklyn audiences travelled to see their own. Kings reversed the direction.\n\nThe booking since has been notably broad — gospel, Caribbean and African acts, comedy, legacy soul, indie rock, orchestral programmes — which reflects an audience the touring industry had historically routed around rather than served.\n\nThere is a straightforward argument that restoring a 1929 movie palace is a nostalgic use of ninety-five million dollars. The counterargument is that the room is full, and that it is full of people who live nearby.",["Restoration took roughly four years","It is among the largest theaters in Brooklyn"],"crowd","story",{}),
("06-09","Les Paul invented multitrack recording in his garage","Rock","1948","Frustrated by one-take recording, Paul rigged a system to layer parts, then built the solid-body electric guitar to reduce feedback.\n\nBoth inventions came from the same impatience. Recording in the forties meant a performance captured in one pass; if you wanted harmony you needed other musicians in the room. Paul wanted to play all the parts himself, so he built a system for recording onto disc and then onto tape while playing along with what was already there — sound on sound, which became multitrack.\n\nHe was doing this in a garage, years before any commercial studio offered it. His records with Mary Ford, with layered guitars and stacked vocals, sounded impossible to contemporaries because the technique did not exist anywhere else.\n\nAmpex built him an eight-track machine in the fifties on his specification, which is essentially the origin of modern studio recording.\n\nThe solid-body guitar came from the same problem-solving. A hollow-body electric feeds back at volume because the body resonates; a solid plank does not. He built a prototype known as the Log and took it to Gibson, who declined, telling him it was a broomstick with a pickup. They changed their minds after Fender proved there was a market.\n\nHe played weekly in New York clubs into his nineties.",["He recorded harmonies with himself years before studios could","Manufacturers initially rejected his guitar design"],"guitar","producer",{}),
("06-10","'Ain't No Half Steppin' announced Brooklyn's technical peak","Hip-Hop","1988","Big Daddy Kane's Bed-Stuy delivery combined speed, clarity and swing, setting a standard other rappers openly chased.\n\nThe technical content is the reason it still gets studied. Kane's delivery combines speed with articulation — most rappers lose one to get the other — and he places multisyllabic rhymes at irregular points in the bar rather than only at the end, which produces internal patterns the ear tracks without being able to predict.\n\nThe breath control is the part MCs mention most. Long passages run without an audible break at a tempo that should require one, and the phrasing swings against the beat rather than sitting on it, which is a jazz instinct applied to speech.\n\nHe was also writing for other people. Biz Markie and Roxanne Shanté both used his material, and he was part of the Juice Crew, which put him in direct competition with the strongest technical rappers of the period on a weekly basis.\n\nThat competitive density is a large part of why the standard rose so fast in the late eighties. Everyone involved was hearing everyone else immediately.\n\nRappers who came after him in the same few Bed-Stuy blocks — Jay-Z most explicitly — have named him as the benchmark they were measuring against before they had recorded anything.",["He wrote for Biz Markie and Roxanne Shanté as well","His breath control is still studied by MCs"],"mic","story",{}),
("06-11","Which 'Superstar' wins: Delaney & Bonnie or the Carpenters?","Pop","1969","Originally a rough rock lament, the Carpenters' 1971 arrangement turned it into a lush, aching ballad with an oboe line.\n\nThe song's authorship is Bonnie Bramlett and Leon Russell, and the early versions — by Delaney & Bonnie, then Rita Coolidge and Bette Midler — treat it as a rock and soul lament, rougher and more direct.\n\nRichard Carpenter reworked it substantially: changed harmony, slowed it, and built an orchestral arrangement around an oboe line that gives the whole record its particular ache. It is a genuinely sophisticated piece of arranging that most listeners experience as simply pretty, which is usually the sign of good work.\n\nKaren Carpenter's vocal is the reason it lands. She sang it from a lyric sheet as a rough guide take, essentially reading it for the first time, and Richard kept that take because nothing subsequent matched it.\n\nThe song's subject is a groupie addressing a musician who does not remember her, which is considerably darker than the Carpenters' public image suggested and is part of why the record has aged better than its reputation.\n\nKaren Carpenter died in 1983 of complications from anorexia nervosa, at thirty-two. Her death did a great deal to bring eating disorders into public discussion at a point when they were barely acknowledged.",["Richard Carpenter reworked the harmony substantially","Karen Carpenter sang the vocal from a rough scratch take that was kept"],"strings","versus",{"a":"Delaney & Bonnie (1969)","b":"The Carpenters (1971)"}),
("06-12","Nile Rodgers wrote 'Le Freak' after being turned away","Disco","1977","Refused entry to Studio 54, Rodgers and Edwards went home angry and wrote a chorus built on an expletive, later changed to 'freak out'.\n\nThey had been invited by Grace Jones and their names were not on the door. New Year's Eve, freezing, and the doormen would not let them in despite Chic being one of the biggest acts in the building's own soundtrack.\n\nThey went back to Rodgers's apartment, started playing, and built a groove with a chorus that was, in its original form, an instruction to the club involving a considerably ruder verb. They kept the melody and the rhythm and substituted 'freak out', which scans identically.\n\nIt became Atlantic Records' biggest-selling single, and it remained so for a very long time.\n\nThe musical content is Chic at their most efficient: Rodgers's muted guitar chucking, Bernard Edwards's melodic bass, Tony Thompson's drums, and almost nothing else. There is enormous space in the arrangement, which is why it still sounds modern.\n\nThe bassline has been sampled and interpolated repeatedly, most famously as the foundation of the Sugarhill Gang's follow-up work and across decades of dance records.\n\nOne of the most successful singles in history exists because two Black musicians were kept standing in the cold outside a nightclub.",["The original hook was considerably ruder","It became Atlantic Records' biggest-selling single"],"guitar","story",{}),
("06-13","Tape delay was invented using two machines","Production","1955","Engineers fed a signal from one tape head to another, creating slapback echo. Sun Studio's version defined early rock and roll vocals.\n\nThe mechanism is entirely physical. A tape machine has separate record and playback heads with a gap between them, so the playback head hears the signal a fraction of a second after it is recorded. Feed that delayed output back into the input and you get a repeating echo, with the delay time determined by the distance between the heads and the speed of the tape.\n\nSam Phillips used a second Ampex machine at Sun to produce the short, single-repeat version known as slapback, and it is all over the early Elvis Presley, Carl Perkins, and Johnny Cash recordings. It gives a voice presence and size without the wash of a reverb chamber, and it is a substantial part of what people mean by the Sun sound.\n\nThe delay could not be set arbitrarily. Changing it meant changing tape speed, which also changed pitch, so engineers worked within a narrow range of usable settings.\n\nPurpose-built tape echo units followed — the Echoplex, the Roland Space Echo — and those became instruments in their own right, particularly in dub, where King Tubby and Lee Perry played the delay as a performance control rather than setting it and leaving it.",["Elvis's voice at Sun is drenched in it","The delay time depended on physical head spacing"],"tape","story",{}),
("06-14","'The Message' proved rap could report the news","Hip-Hop","1982","Grandmaster Flash and the Furious Five released a bleak social document at a time when rap was expected to be party music.\n\nThe expectation in 1982 was firm. Rap records were party records, and the commercial logic was that nobody would dance to a description of a collapsing neighbourhood. Most of the group agreed and did not want to record it.\n\nMelle Mel delivers nearly all of it, and Grandmaster Flash barely appears despite the billing. The song was largely written by Duke Bootee, a Sugar Hill session musician, with Melle Mel contributing a verse he had used previously.\n\nWhat it established was that the form could carry reportage. The lyric describes a specific set of conditions — debt, decay, violence, the absence of any exit — with a plainness that had no precedent on a rap record, and the closing scene of the album version is genuinely bleak.\n\nIt was a commercial success, which settled the argument about whether audiences would accept it.\n\nEverything from Public Enemy to Nas to Kendrick Lamar runs through this record, and the internal resistance to making it is a useful reminder that the people inside a form are frequently the last to know what it can do.",["Most of the group disliked the song initially","Melle Mel delivers nearly all of it"],"turntable","story",{}),
("06-15","Ditmas Park's block parties keep the sound-system tradition","Brooklyn","1995","Summer street closures along Cortelyou and Rugby Road turned into speaker-stack parties, echoing Caribbean sound-system culture nearby.\n\nThe mechanics are bureaucratic and the results are not. A block association applies to the city for a street activity permit, the street closes for a day, and what fills it is determined entirely by who lives there. In this part of Brooklyn that has meant, reliably, speaker stacks and a generator.\n\nThe lineage is direct. Central Brooklyn holds one of the largest Caribbean populations outside the islands, and Jamaican sound system practice — the mobile rig, the operator, the bass pushed far past what indoor listening requires — arrived with the migration and never left. A Cortelyou block party is running the same format as a Kingston yard dance, adapted to a street permit.\n\nThe housing stock helps. Detached houses with front lawns and porches mean a block can absorb amplified music in a way a wall-to-wall tenement street cannot.\n\nDJs still run generators for outdoor sets because there is no other reliable power, which is its own continuity with the tradition.\n\nIt is also, quietly, one of the few remaining occasions when an entire block is outside at the same time and has to negotiate volume, space, and each other in person.",["Permits are typically pulled by block associations","DJs still run generators for outdoor sets"],"crowd","story",{}),
("06-16","Tupac studied ballet and Shakespeare","Hip-Hop","1971","Shakur trained at the Baltimore School for the Arts, performing in ballet and theatre before rapping professionally.\n\nThe training was serious. He studied acting, poetry, and dance at a competitive arts high school, performed Shakespeare, and danced the Mouse King in a production of The Nutcracker. He later described those years as the most formative of his life and said the exposure to people from entirely different backgrounds changed what he thought was possible.\n\nHe credited the acting training directly for his delivery. What separates his recorded vocals from most of his contemporaries is not technical rhyme construction but performance — dynamics, timing, and an ability to shift register within a verse that comes from stage work rather than from rapping.\n\nHis mother Afeni Shakur was a Black Panther who defended herself successfully in a major conspiracy trial while pregnant with him, which is the other half of the formation.\n\nThe contradiction between the arts-school background and the public persona was frequently pointed out and he never had much difficulty holding both.\n\nHe was murdered in Las Vegas in September 1996, at twenty-five. The case remains unresolved, and the volume of unreleased material he left behind means more albums were issued after his death than during his life.",["He played the Mouse King in The Nutcracker","He credited acting training for his delivery"],"mic","story",{}),
("06-17","The Linn drum machine used real recorded drums","Pop","1980","Roger Linn sampled actual drum hits instead of synthesizing them, giving eighties pop its crisp, oversized snare.\n\nEvery drum machine before it synthesized its sounds, which is why they were used as accompaniment devices for organists rather than as replacements for drummers. Linn's insight was to record actual drum hits to digital memory and trigger those, which meant a machine could sound like a kit for the first time.\n\nThe LM-1 also had programmable patterns and swing, so a producer could shift the timing off the grid slightly and get something that felt played rather than quantised.\n\nIt cost around five thousand dollars in 1980, roughly the price of a car, and only a few hundred were built. Its early users were consequently the people who could afford it, which is why a particular snare sound is all over expensive eighties pop.\n\nPrince built a substantial part of his sound on it — the drums on much of his early-eighties work are LM-1, frequently with the pitch and decay pushed to extremes.\n\nThe successor, the LinnDrum, was cheaper and spread further. Between them they defined a decade's rhythm sound, and their gated, oversized snare is now instant shorthand for the period.",["Prince built much of his sound on the LM-1","It cost roughly as much as a car"],"drums","producer",{}),
("06-18","Paul McCartney woke up with 'Yesterday' in his head","Rock","1942","McCartney dreamed the melody and spent weeks asking people if he had accidentally stolen it. Placeholder lyrics were 'scrambled eggs'.\n\nHe woke in a room at his girlfriend's family home with the tune complete in his head, went to a piano, and played it before he lost it. His immediate assumption was that it must be somebody else's — a melody that arrives fully formed feels like a memory rather than an invention — and he spent weeks playing it to people in the industry asking whether they recognised it.\n\nNobody did, so he kept it.\n\nThe placeholder lyric was 'scrambled eggs, oh my baby how I love your legs', which scans correctly and is the sort of thing that makes the finished song slightly harder to take seriously once you know it.\n\nThe recording is unusual for a Beatles track: McCartney alone with an acoustic guitar and a string quartet arranged by George Martin, with no other Beatle on it. Martin has said McCartney was initially resistant to the strings, worried it would sound like easy listening.\n\nIt is among the most covered songs ever written, with recorded versions running into the thousands.\n\nMcCartney has said he has never had another melody arrive that way, which is probably just as well.",["It is among the most covered songs ever written","He recorded it alone with a string quartet"],"strings","story",{}),
("06-19","Juneteenth and the blues' long road north","Blues","1865","Emancipation's spread carried musical traditions from the Deep South up through Memphis and Chicago, reshaping American popular music.\n\nJuneteenth marks 19 June 1865, when Union troops reached Galveston, Texas, and enforced the Emancipation Proclamation — two and a half years after it had been issued. That gap is the point of the commemoration: freedom arriving late, and only where it could be enforced.\n\nThe musical consequence took decades and ran along the railway lines. The Great Migration moved roughly six million people out of the rural South between 1916 and 1970, and the music travelled with them — Delta blues up the Illinois Central to Memphis and Chicago, Texas blues west to California.\n\nWhat changed on arrival was volume. Country blues was made for a porch or a small room and worked acoustically. Chicago clubs were loud, crowded, and full of competing noise, which required amplification, and amplification changed the music rather than simply enlarging it: distorted guitar, harmonica through a small overdriven amp, and a full rhythm section replacing a single player.\n\nMuddy Waters is the clearest single example of the transition, recorded acoustically in Mississippi in 1941 and electrically in Chicago within a decade.\n\nEverything from rock and roll to soul to hip-hop runs back through that migration.",["The Great Migration moved the blues to electric amplification","Chicago's louder clubs demanded volume country blues never needed"],"guitar","story",{}),
("06-20","Brian Wilson built 'Pet Sounds' while the band toured","Pop","1966","Wilson stopped touring and recorded the album with session players, presenting finished tracks to the Beach Boys on their return.\n\nHe had stopped touring in 1964 after a panic attack on a flight, which turned out to be the most productive decision of his career. While the band worked the road, he had unlimited studio time in Los Angeles and access to the session players later known as the Wrecking Crew.\n\nHe built the album as a producer rather than as a bandleader — writing parts, directing musicians, and constructing arrangements piece by piece across many sessions. The band returned to finished instrumental tracks and were asked to sing on them.\n\nSome of them resisted. Mike Love in particular was, by most accounts, unconvinced by material that abandoned the surf and car songs the group had been selling successfully.\n\nThe instrumentation is deliberately strange: bicycle bells, dog whistles, empty Coca-Cola bottles, theremin-like electro-theremin, and unconventional doublings that produce composite timbres you cannot identify.\n\nIt sold less well than its predecessors in America and enormously influenced everyone who heard it, including Paul McCartney, who has repeatedly said it prompted Sgt. Pepper.\n\nWilson's mental health deteriorated badly in the years afterward. He died in 2025.",["He used bicycle bells, dog whistles and empty bottles","Some bandmates initially resisted the material"],"mixer","producer",{}),
("06-21","'Funky Worm' gave G-funk its whine","Funk","1972","The Ohio Players' high synthesizer squeal became the signature lead sound of West Coast hip-hop two decades later.\n\nThe original is a joke. The track is a comic novelty built around a character called the worm, and the high, wavering synthesizer lead — played on an Arp — was intended as a cartoonish effect rather than a musical statement.\n\nTwenty years later Dr. Dre and his contemporaries lifted that timbre and made it the defining lead sound of West Coast hip-hop. It appears on N.W.A records, across The Chronic, and throughout Snoop Dogg's early work, usually played rather than sampled but unmistakably derived from the same source.\n\nThe reason it works in that context is that it occupies a frequency range nothing else in the mix uses. Under a heavy 808 or live-feeling bass and a mid-range vocal, a thin high whine cuts through without any competition, which is why G-funk arrangements sound so wide.\n\nThe Ohio Players were a serious funk band with a substantial catalogue, and being remembered principally for a comic synthesizer squeal is a strange fate.\n\nIt is a good example of how sampling culture reassigns value. The thing later producers wanted was the element the original band would have considered least important.",["Dr. Dre used the tone repeatedly","The original was meant as a comic effect"],"synth","sample",{"src":"Ohio Players - Funky Worm (1972)","hits":["N.W.A - Dopeman","Dr. Dre - Let Me Ride","Snoop Dogg - Who Am I"]}),
("06-22","Which 'Valerie' wins: The Zutons or Amy Winehouse?","Pop","2006","The Zutons wrote it as indie rock. Mark Ronson and Amy Winehouse recut it as sixties soul, and that version eclipsed the original.\n\nThe Zutons' original is a Liverpool indie rock record — guitar-driven, mid-tempo, with a saxophone and a slightly ragged charm. It was a modest hit and is, on its own terms, a good song.\n\nRonson's rearrangement moves it wholesale into mid-sixties soul: a Motown-derived backbeat, horn section, tambourine, and an arrangement that sounds like it was cut at a Detroit session in 1966. Nothing in the melody changes.\n\nWinehouse recorded the vocal quickly and largely live, and what she does with it is the reason the version took over. She sings behind the beat throughout, with a phrasing derived from jazz singers rather than pop ones, and treats the lyric conversationally rather than performing it.\n\nThat combination — modern songwriting, period arrangement, and a singer with a genuinely pre-rock sense of time — is the whole formula of Back to Black and of a wave of records that followed it.\n\nWinehouse died in 2011, at twenty-seven, of alcohol poisoning after years of very public struggle that the British press covered as entertainment.\n\nThe Zutons' version is now the one people are surprised exists.",["Ronson built the arrangement around a Motown-style backbeat","Winehouse recorded her vocal quickly, largely live"],"horn","versus",{"a":"The Zutons (2006)","b":"Amy Winehouse & Mark Ronson (2007)"}),
("06-23","Bessie Smith outsold nearly every artist of her era","Blues","1923","Smith's records sold in the hundreds of thousands to Black audiences largely ignored by the mainstream industry.\n\nThe commercial scale is the part that gets understated. Her records sold in the hundreds of thousands at a time when the industry had assumed no significant Black record-buying market existed, and her success — following Mamie Smith's 'Crazy Blues' in 1920 — is what created the race records category and the entire structure of Black popular recording that followed.\n\nShe toured in her own railroad car, which was both a display of success and a practical necessity: segregated travel across the South made ordinary touring dangerous and frequently impossible, and owning the car meant the company could sleep and move without depending on facilities that would not accept them.\n\nThe voice itself required no microphone. She recorded acoustically for much of her career and could fill a tent without amplification, with a power and precision of pitch that is audible even through the limitations of 1920s recording.\n\nHer material was frank about sex, drinking, violence, and independence in ways that later decades became squeamish about.\n\nShe died in 1937 after a car accident in Mississippi. The long-repeated story that she was refused treatment at a whites-only hospital appears not to be accurate, though the circumstances of her death were shaped by segregation regardless.",["She toured in her own railroad car","Her success proved a market executives had denied existed"],"mic","story",{}),
("06-24","The Neve console became a producer's instrument","Production","1970","Rupert Neve's circuitry added a warmth engineers still chase. Studios built entire reputations on owning one.\n\nNeve designed to measurable specifications rather than to a sound. His stated aim was accuracy — low noise, wide bandwidth, minimal distortion — and the console's celebrated warmth is largely a by-product of the transformers and discrete class-A circuitry he used to achieve it. The harmonic content those components add is technically a departure from accuracy that happens to be flattering.\n\nThe 1073 preamp module in particular became so sought-after that individual units now sell for several thousand dollars each, and studios have been bought and stripped for their consoles.\n\nWhat that produced is an unusual situation where a piece of infrastructure became a creative signature. Records were booked into specific rooms because of the desk, and engineers developed techniques built around its particular behaviour when driven hard.\n\nSoftware emulations now model not just the design but specific physical units, down to serial numbers, because component tolerances mean two consoles off the same production line do not sound identical.\n\nThe underlying lesson is one the audio industry keeps relearning. Every generation's idea of clean becomes the next generation's idea of character, and the flaws engineers spent decades removing get carefully reinstated as plugins.",["Plugins now emulate specific serial numbers","Neve designed to a spec, not a sound, and got both"],"mixer","producer",{}),
("06-25","Michael Jackson died, and streaming spiked worldwide","Pop","2009","Jackson's death caused traffic surges that briefly destabilized major websites, an early sign of how music news moved online.\n\nThe load was genuinely unprecedented for a single news event. Google's search team initially interpreted the volume of queries as an automated attack and served error pages; Twitter went down; Wikipedia and several news sites buckled. AOL's instant messaging service failed for a period.\n\nIt was an early demonstration that the internet's capacity planning assumed distributed interest rather than the entire connected world wanting the same thing in the same ten minutes.\n\nThe commercial aftermath was equally striking. His catalogue sold in enormous quantities immediately, and he became for several years the highest-earning deceased performer by a wide margin.\n\nThriller remains the best-selling album ever released, and the sales figures for it have the odd property of being disputed largely upward.\n\nJackson died of acute propofol intoxication; his physician was later convicted of involuntary manslaughter.\n\nThe allegations of child sexual abuse made against him during his life and after his death have not gone away, and how to hold his work and those allegations together at once is a question the culture has handled poorly in both directions — alternately ignoring the accusations entirely and pretending the music never mattered.",["Some services mistook the traffic for an attack","'Thriller' remains the best-selling album ever"],"mic","story",{}),
("06-26","Sly Stone assembled America's first fully integrated band","Funk","1967","Sly and the Family Stone included Black and white members, men and women, all sharing lead vocals, unprecedented at the time.\n\nThe composition of the band was the argument. In 1967 popular music was segregated by convention at almost every level — audiences, radio formats, touring circuits, and the personnel of the bands themselves — and a group that was mixed by race and by gender, with lead vocals passed around rather than assigned to a frontman, was making a claim simply by walking on stage.\n\nCynthia Robinson on trumpet was one of very few women playing a lead horn in a major American band, and she was not decorative — she plays and shouts across the records constantly.\n\nThe music enacted the same idea. Everyone sings, the arrangements pass melodic responsibility between members mid-song, and the sound fuses soul, rock, gospel, and psychedelia without treating any of them as the home key.\n\nSly Stone had worked as a radio DJ and a staff producer before forming the band, which is part of why the records are so structurally assured.\n\nIt did not hold. Drug use, pressure from militants who objected to the white members, and Stone's deteriorating condition pulled the group apart within a few years, and There's a Riot Goin' On is the sound of that collapse.\n\nStone died in 2025.",["Trumpeter Cynthia Robinson was a rare female horn lead","The lineup was itself a political statement"],"horn","story",{}),
("06-27","'Nautilus' became crate-diggers' holy grail","Jazz","1974","Bob James's track hid a haunting synth figure that producers mined for decades, often unaware others had found it too.\n\nJames was a jazz keyboardist making accessible instrumental records for CTI, and 'Nautilus' closes an album otherwise full of considerably lighter material. It is slow, minor, and built on a synthesizer figure with an unsettled quality that has almost nothing in common with the rest of his catalogue.\n\nThat sound turned out to be perfect raw material. It is harmonically ambiguous enough to sit under anything, texturally distinctive enough to be recognisable, and slow enough to be sped up without artefacts.\n\nGhostface Killah, Slick Rick, Run-DMC, Eric B. & Rakim, and dozens of others built records on it, frequently within the same few years and without knowing others were working from the same source.\n\nJames has been unusually gracious about all of it, saying he is delighted by the second life and pointing out that a track buried at the end of a 1974 jazz-funk album would otherwise have been forgotten entirely.\n\nThat attitude is rarer than it should be. A number of jazz musicians whose work underpins hip-hop treated the borrowing as theft, which is legally defensible and has generally made them poorer company than the people who leaned into it.",["It anchors multiple hip-hop classics","James has said he is delighted by its second life"],"synth","sample",{"src":"Bob James - Nautilus (1974)","hits":["Ghostface Killah - Daytona 500","Slick Rick - Children's Story","Run-DMC - Beats to the Rhyme"]}),
("06-28","Gil Scott-Heron read poetry over congas","Soul","1970","'The Revolution Will Not Be Televised' was spoken word over percussion, an ancestor of rap that Scott-Heron resisted being called.\n\nThe first recorded version, on Small Talk at 125th and Lenox, is voice and congas and nothing else. A later version added a full band and is the one most people know, but the original makes the point more sharply: the words carry the entire piece and the percussion is a pulse rather than an accompaniment.\n\nThe text is a catalogue of advertising slogans, television personalities, and consumer imagery, arranged to make an argument about the difference between watching change and participating in it. It is genuinely funny in places, which people who quote the title tend to forget.\n\nHe resisted being called a father of rap, and the objection was substantive rather than snobbish. He located his work in a longer tradition — Langston Hughes, the Last Poets, blues, and jazz — and preferred the term bluesologist, a word he coined for himself.\n\nHe was also, by his own account, wary of being made into a founding ancestor for a commercial form he had complicated feelings about.\n\nHis later years involved addiction and imprisonment. The final album, made shortly before his death in 2011, is one of the bleakest and best things he did.",["He preferred the term 'bluesologist'","The first version used only congas and voice"],"drums","writer",{}),
("06-29","Little Richard's 'Tutti Frutti' had to be rewritten on the spot","Rock","1955","The original lyrics were far too explicit for radio, so a local songwriter rewrote them during a lunch break at the session.\n\nThe session was going badly. Richard had been cutting conventional blues numbers at J&M in New Orleans and none of it was working, and during a break at a nearby club he sat at a piano and played the song as he actually performed it live — which was explicitly about gay sex and completely unreleasable in 1955.\n\nProducer Bumps Blackwell recognised the energy immediately and brought in Dorothy LaBostrie, a local songwriter, who rewrote the lyric in roughly fifteen minutes over lunch. The result is nonsense about girls named Sue and Daisy, and the nonsense is precisely why it works — the words carry no meaning, so all the information is in the delivery.\n\nThe opening piano and the shouted syllables were kept exactly as first played.\n\nWhat that record established is difficult to overstate: the pounding piano, the screamed vocal, and the sheer velocity became the template for rock and roll performance.\n\nPat Boone's sanitised cover outsold it, which was standard practice for white artists covering Black records at the time and is one of the clearer illustrations of how the money moved.\n\nRichard was, throughout, considerably queerer than the record industry was willing to let him be.",["The rewrite took roughly 15 minutes","Richard's piano intro was kept exactly as first played"],"piano","story",{}),
("06-30","Brooklyn's Celebrate Brooklyn keeps free music alive","Brooklyn","1979","The Prospect Park bandshell series has run for over four decades, one of the last major free festivals in New York.\n\nIt began in a city that was effectively broke. New York in 1979 had come through near-bankruptcy, park budgets had collapsed, and Prospect Park had a reputation that kept people out of it after dark. Free concerts at the bandshell were partly cultural programming and partly a deliberate attempt to give surrounding neighbourhoods a reason to reclaim the space.\n\nMore than four decades on it is among the longest-running free outdoor performance festivals in the country, and the booking has stayed genuinely broad — Afrobeat, salsa, indie rock, jazz, classical, hip-hop, and film screenings with live scores.\n\nThe bandshell's position at Prospect Park West and Ninth Street puts it within walking distance of Ditmas Park, Flatbush, Windsor Terrace, Park Slope, and Crown Heights, which is why the crowd looks like the borough rather than like one neighbourhood.\n\nMost of the programme remains free, funded partly by a small number of ticketed benefit shows.\n\nA good proportion of the artists who play it go on to headline paid rooms elsewhere in the city, which makes it one of the few places where you can see something significant before the ticket price catches up.",["It draws crowds from across Flatbush and Ditmas Park","Many artists play it before headlining paid venues"],"crowd","story",{}),
]
